WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.200
Read the publishing architecture by following
one request rather than counting boxes. A

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:10.280
visitor asks for a memorial page. The web
application queries structured records in a

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:14.403
relational database and returns HTML containing
links to photographs.

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:19.400
The browser then requests those media objects
separately, potentially through a content-

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:24.355
delivery network. A background scraper gathers
candidate information for later use, while

00:00:24.355 --> 00:00:29.478
administrative users edit content through
authenticated endpoints. These are different
paths

00:00:29.478 --> 00:00:31.983
with different timing and trust requirements.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.722
The application can remain a stateless monolith
even though it uses several external

00:00:36.722 --> 00:00:41.500
services: architectural scalability does not
require splitting every function into an

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:44.479
independently deployed microservice at the
beginning.

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:49.580
Now follow an administrative change. The request
must identify the user and check

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:55.036
authorization before modifying records. The
database owns structured facts such as page

00:00:55.036 --> 00:01:00.680
details, photo relationships, comments and edit
history. The media store owns image bytes.

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:05.516
A background task and a public read can
therefore touch different components without all

00:01:05.516 --> 00:01:10.572
work passing through the same synchronous chain.
Draw public reads and administrative writes

00:01:10.572 --> 00:01:15.518
as distinct arrows into the application, then
identify which data each operation accesses.

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:20.624
This makes the interfaces meaningful. A label
such as database says little until we know

00:01:20.624 --> 00:01:25.175
whether an arrow performs a lookup, commits a
change or transfers a large object.

00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:30.374
Photographs are stored as objects, with derived
sizes for different uses. A page showing a

00:01:30.374 --> 00:01:35.453
small thumbnail should not force every visitor
to download the original full-size image.

00:01:35.453 --> 00:01:40.706
Generating reusable variants trades some
processing and storage for lower repeated
transfer

00:01:40.706 --> 00:01:42.091
cost and faster display.

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:47.347
The application can return a media URL while the
browser fetches the bytes directly from the

00:01:47.347 --> 00:01:52.457
delivery path. This separates the cost of
constructing a page from the cost of
transferring

00:01:52.457 --> 00:01:57.568
its images. The diagram should show one source
image producing several named variants, then

00:01:57.568 --> 00:02:00.151
a page choosing the size it actually displays.

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:04.532
This is a concrete example of moving repeated
work out of the request path.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:09.901
A content-delivery network places reusable media
nearer users and reduces repeated requests

00:02:09.901 --> 00:02:15.122
to the origin. It is not free, and a high hit
rate does not mean there is no delivery bill:

00:02:15.122 --> 00:02:17.761
bytes still travel from the cache to visitors.

00:02:17.761 --> 00:02:23.382
The case's historical cost breakdown shows why
architecture discussions need measurements of

00:02:23.382 --> 00:02:29.003
both demand and spending. Compute instances,
database capacity, object storage, requests and

00:02:29.003 --> 00:02:34.257
data transfer are separate cost drivers. A fast
media path can dominate cost even when

00:02:34.257 --> 00:02:36.090
application servers are small.

00:02:36.261 --> 00:02:41.410
Treat the historical dollar amounts as evidence
about that particular workload and time, not

00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:45.329
as current prices or a universal budget for
another publishing system.
